I've just finished the game and after some time reflection this is my review:
Pros:
- Great, funny and childish story with some mature and brutal moments;
- Large, atmospheric and detailed areas to explore;
- The combat was ok, you unlock different combos throughout the game;
- Different gadgets to use;
- Fun side quest;
- Classes were really fun and rewarding ( Clothes and bonuses);
- There was a good sence of progression ( new zones would unlock, your room gets special trophys);
- There were different seasons and factions ( like nerds; jocks, bullies, etc)
- Fun and memorable characters;
Cons:
- I felt the driving was a bit clunky ( that's a shame, since that's your only option to get anywhere);
- There were some camera problems;
- Trying to fight +2 npcs would be pretty terrible since you would miss or hit the wrong one;
- There was no separation between the main and side quest so you had no idea which one you were playing ( but you can still finish them at the end of the story);
Final verdic:
Bully is a really good, funny and memorable game.
It may be a little old, the graphics and combat a bit outdated but It still deserves a 8/10.

Considering technical aspects, World of Tanks is far superior and a true PC game.
- Exclusive full-screen mode (G-Sync compatible);
- Is possible to set refresh-rate (up to 144Hz);
- Can choose video ouput device (play on monitor or TV whenever I want);
- DirectX 11 (vs. DirectX 9 on WoT Blitz), resulting in better resources management, higher frame-rate, SLI scaling, GPU usage;
- Surround sound system support (I use 5.1).
Advantages of WoT Blitz:
- Some exclusive contents (ex.: Valkyria Chronicles tanks);
- Switch between regions without creating multiple accounts;
- Available on Steam (because they want to make it more popular);
- Cross-platform (playing on PC against casual Android/iOS players).
For better gaming experience, I prefer WoT. Blitz is a poor mobile platform port.

TL;DR: If you're on the fence about this one, I definitely recommend it. It's hard to get used to, but it can actually be very powerful.
Well, this was a tough decision. I was thinking about buying this for a long time, and eventually I got tired of trying (unsuccessfully) to build a local kart track using blender, so I thought I'd give this a try (even with all the bad reviews), and if I wasn't able to do it quickly, I'd get a refund. Long story short, after googling a lot, reading a lot of posts on the forums here, and watching a couple of youtube videos (side note: I HATE video tutorials), I was able to set-up the google maps api, download the terrain data and create a very very very simple track based on the real one I wanted in under one hour. I could still get the refund, but I was quite happy with the result, so I decided to keep it.
So, what I liked about it:
- You can easily create tracks;
- You can create a track based on a real one, or a city circuit from a couple of roads you usually drive on;
- With patience, any real world location can become a track;
- Lots of objects (now with Workshop support).
What I didn't like:
- Awful interface (a pinning option for the menus and toolbars would almost solve this);
- Not so intuitive controls;
- Slow at times (good habits can help with this);
- Editing tools that feel "incomplete". For example, the lack of bezier handles, track width (point by point only), and better intersection tools;
- Really hard to find learning references. As I mentioned, I HATE video tutorials. It'd be really nice to have a complete written guide to all the steps needed to successfully create and export a track. I plan to do this once I get a bit more used to everything.
Even with all its flaws, the software is still good and quite powerful, so get it if you really want to create tracks!

